  "prediction": "The Department of Defense (DOD) establishes foreign currency budget rates to determine its funding needs and calculate gains or losses from overseas expenditures. The report examines DOD's use of foreign currency budget rates and its management of the Foreign Currency Fluctuations Defense (FCFD) account.\n\nDOD has revised its foreign currency budget rates since 2009 to more closely align with market rates. The revisions have decreased DOD's projected O&M and MILPERS funding needs and potential gains or losses due to foreign currency fluctuations. However, DOD has not consistently selected the most cost-effective foreign currency rates for disbursements, resulting in potential cost savings opportunities.\n\nThe FCFD account is used to cover losses due to foreign currency fluctuations, but DOD has not effectively managed the account balance based on projected gains or losses. DOD has maintained the FCFD account balance at the maximum level allowed by statute, which may be higher than necessary to cover losses. This has resulted in unobligated balances being transferred into the FCFD account without consideration of projected losses.\n\nThe report also highlights issues with DOD's financial reporting on foreign currency fluctuations. The Army's Foreign Currency Fluctuations Defense (O&M) reports are inaccurate, and the Navy's reports contain incomplete data. The Defense Finance and Accounting Service has not corrected these issues, and guidance is needed to ensure complete and accurate data are included in financial reporting.\n\nThe report makes four recommendations to DOD:\n\n1. Conduct a review of foreign currency rates used at disbursement to determine cost-saving opportunities.\n2. Analyze realized and projected losses to determine the necessary size of the FCFD account balance.\n3. Revise the Financial Management Regulation to include guidance on ensuring complete and accurate data in Foreign Currency Fluctuations Defense (O&M) reports.\n4. Develop a plan with timelines for implementing changes to the Army's General Fund Enterprise Business System to accurately record disbursements.\n\nDOD has partially concurred with the second recommendation, citing the difficulty in forecasting foreign currency rates due to market volatility. However, the report emphasizes that analyzing actual and projected losses would provide opportunities to more efficiently use unobligated balances for other defense activities or return them to Treasury.",
